{"reference":"Job 23","book":"Job","chapter":23,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Job’s Place in the Debate","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job’s speeches come after his friends first sit with him silently for seven days, then begin a sustained debate in which Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar increasingly explain his suffering as proof of wrongdoing. Job rejects that diagnosis. This wider setting clarifies why his desire to present his case before God is so urgent: he is not accepting his friends’ account of him.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_023_f5208d4dd18f07f907312c57"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Later reflection associates Job with suffering, patience, endurance, and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. In this larger sense, Job’s steadfastness can describe persistence through the whole story rather than silent acceptance of every circumstance; it should not be used to require someone to remain in abuse or to forgo escape and protection.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_023_622904f56ef203977a92ef66"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}